            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the slope of an array at every point: how fast the values are changing there. Inside the array it takes the centred difference - half the distance from the value before to the value after - and at the two ends the one-sided difference.\n\nUnlike np.diff, the result is the same length as the input and each value is centred on its own point, not between two. That makes it the natural way to get velocities from a recorded path (and accelerations from those), slopes from a curve, or edges from a row of an image, while keeping everything lined up with the original.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.gradient <spacing: float> <axis: int>\nDefaults: spacing 1, axis -1 (along each row).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.gradient 0.01667\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is differentiated and sent.\n\nspacing (float box, also an inlet):\nThe distance between neighbouring values. The slopes are divided by it, so with the time between samples (1/60 for 60 frames a second) the result is per second.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to measure: -1 along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nedge order:\nAn option, under the node: 1 (the default) or 2. 2 uses three points at each end, which follows curves more closely there.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ngradient:\nThe slope at each point, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe squares 0 1 4 9 16 25 are a path speeding up. np.gradient gives 1 2 4 6 8 9: at each point, its speed. Inside, these are exactly twice the position's index, as they should be for squares; the two ends are rougher. np.diff gives 1 3 5 7 9 - the steps BETWEEN points, one fewer. A metro re-sends the path five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.diff gives the steps between neighbours. np.cumsum goes the other way, from steps back to a path. diff measures change in a stream, one sample at a time.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 840,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the slope of an array at every point: how fast the values are changing there. Inside the array it takes the centred difference - half the distance from the value before to the value after - and at the two ends the one-sided difference.\n\nUnlike np.diff, the result is the same length as the input and each value is centred on its own point, not between two. That makes it the natural way to get velocities from a recorded path (and accelerations from those), slopes from a curve, or edges from a row of an image, while keeping everything lined up with the original.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.gradient <spacing: float> <axis: int>\nDefaults: spacing 1, axis -1 (along each row).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.gradient 0.01667\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is differentiated and sent.\n\nspacing (float box, also an inlet):\nThe distance between neighbouring values. The slopes are divided by it, so with the time between samples (1/60 for 60 frames a second) the result is per second.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to measure: -1 along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nedge order:\nAn option, under the node: 1 (the default) or 2. 2 uses three points at each end, which follows curves more closely there.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ngradient:\nThe slope at each point, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe squares 0 1 4 9 16 25 are a path speeding up. np.gradient gives 1 2 4 6 8 9: at each point, its speed. Inside, these are exactly twice the position's index, as they should be for squares; the two ends are rougher. np.diff gives 1 3 5 7 9 - the steps BETWEEN points, one fewer. A metro re-sends the path five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.diff gives the steps between neighbours. np.cumsum goes the other way, from steps back to a path. diff measures change in a stream, one sample at a time."
        },
